# The environment file every `bun` process the e2e harness starts is pinned to
# (GH #231). It is deliberately EMPTY of assignments, and it must stay that way.
#
# `bun` auto-loads the `.env` of its working directory, and the harness runs its
# servers from the repository root — so a developer's own `.env` reached the app
# server under test and reconfigured it. That is how a run once had the mail
# backend URL (STALWART_URL then, JMAP_URL since GH #33) pointing at a REMOTE
# mail host: /api/health degraded to 503, Playwright waited out its 120s on a
# server that was never going to be ready, and not one spec executed. Every
# other variable the server reads (AI_*, METRICS_TOKEN, SESSION_TTL_HOURS,
# JMAP_URL_MODE, JMAP_AUTH_MODE, MASTER_KEY_*, …) leaked the same way, just
# without announcing itself.
#
# Passing `--env-file` replaces that auto-load rather than adding to it, so this
# file being empty is the whole point: the servers under test see exactly the
# environment playwright.config.ts hands them and nothing else.
#
# Do NOT configure the harness here. Anything a server needs belongs in
# `appServerEnv` (or the per-server `env` block) in playwright.config.ts, where
# it is visible next to the server it configures. A run must produce the same
# result on a machine whose `.env` points at production as on one with no `.env`
# at all.
#
# Named `app-server.env` rather than `.env.something` on purpose: `.gitignore`
# excludes `.env*`, and a file that does not exist in a fresh checkout would
# leave CI with no isolation at all.
